  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain chances and gusty winds look to linger along the coast today \nas the nor'easter continues to track offshore. \n\nLatest surface analysis shows the nor'easter continuing to deepen \noffshore, now with a ~1004 mb surface low. Strong Canadian high \npressure ~1032 mb remains centered over eastern Canada. Cooler \ntemperatures, windy conditions, and rain chances continue today in \nresult of these two major systems. Additionally, potentially \ndangerous conditions like dangerous surf, beach erosion, and \nmoderate to major tidal flooding will continue for the next few days \nnear the coast.\n\nThe nor'easter will slide further north today with high pressure \ntrying to build in from the northwest. This will allow cloud cover \nto clear some in the piedmont with partly cloudy skies. Meanwhile at \nthe coast, thick cloud cover and intermittent drizzle will persist \non the backside of that low. Temperatures today will remain cool \nwith highs around 70F across the area. Breezy winds will continue \nwith gusts to 20-30 mph (highest along the coast), but shy of Wind \nAdvisory criteria. \n\nThe 00z global models came into better agreement with the track of \nthe nor'easter over the next couple of days, with both the GFS and \nECMWF taking the center of the low into New Jersey on Sunday before \nbringing it back offshore by early next week. This brings higher \nconfidence in windy conditions and lingering drizzle Saturday into \nSunday. Winds will shift out of the NW with gusts of 25-35 mph \n(higher gusts possible on the Eastern Shore). High temperatures will \nhave a large range across the area Saturday with cooler temps in the \nmid to upper 60s on the Eastern Shore and along the coast to warmer \ntemps in south central VA and inland NE NC reaching the mid 70s. \nMost inland locations will see highs in the lower 70s though. A \nsimilar gradient will be evident on Sunday.\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region late Monday through Wednesday \ngiving us a break with dry weather and sunshine. Temperatures will \nbe able to warm more each day with the ridging overhead. On Monday, \nexpect low to mid 70s for the north and upper 70s to near 80 degrees \nacross the south. By mid-week, we may be well above normal once \nagain (mid 80s) as southerly flow develops ahead of the next cold \nfront. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread moderate to locally major tidal flooding \n(inundation of 2-3 feet AGL in vulnerable areas) will continue \nthrough tomorrow night at most spots, with some locations especially \nin the middle Bay potentially seeing moderate coastal flooding \nthrough Saturday or Sunday.\n\nAs low pressure develops off the coast, winds have backed slightly \ntoday over the region, now closer to North-Northeast instead of NE. \nIn addition, the speeds have also decreased slightly at 20-30 kt \nwith gusts to 35 kt. However, this has done little to change the \ncoastal flood situation with most sites currently seeing anomalies \nof 2.5-3.5 ft above normal astronomical tide today. With little \nchange overnight into Friday, expect similar anomalies tonight and \neven into Friday. These anomalies combined with higher astronomical \ntides through tomorrow will likely keep similar flooding as has been \nexperienced for the last few high tide cycles. As such, have opted \nto extend the coastal flood warning for all sites through Friday \nnight. \n\nOverall, storm surge guidance has trended higher with the 12z runs \ninside the bay Saturday and even Sunday. This is likely due to the \nmodels depicting the offshore low pressure potentially being \nslightly stronger and further west than previous runs. While this \nwill lead to a more N-NW flow inside the bay leading to more ebbing \nat the mouth of the Bay, it is also possible that especially the \neastern side of the Bay could see increased water levels with a \nbetter onshore component to the winds. As such, have opted to \nincrease the tides slightly into Saturday for the middle Bay and \neastern side of the bay. There is still uncertainty in the tide \nforecast for the weekend, so we will not extend the warnings into \nthe weekend at this time but it is quite possible that some warnings \nwill need to be extended.",
